.TH SIFT 1
.SH NAME
sift \- find a pattern of tokens
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sift
[
.B -l
.I lang
] [
.B -n
]
.I pattern
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sift
finds a pattern of lexical tokens, not of characters, in each
.IR file .
White space and comments do not count, so a match can span lines.
For each file,
.I sift
runs the syntax program that the rules in
.B $home/lib/syn
or else
.B /lib/syn
give for its name, sends it the file's text with the request
.BR body ,
asks for its tokens with
.BR tokens ,
and matches the pattern against them.
A rule is an
.IR ndb (6)
entry:
.B syn=
names the language,
.B cmd=
is an
.IR rc (1)
command that runs the program, and
.B file=
is a regular expression that the file's name must match.
The first entry whose
.B file=
matches wins.
.I Sift
reports a file that no rule covers and goes on to the next.
With no files,
.I sift
reads its standard input, whose language
.B -l
names;
.B -l
also names the language of every file given.
.PP
.I Sift
prints each match as an address,
.IB file :# q0 ,# q1\f1,
in runes, as
.IR sam (1)
and
.IR plumb (1)
take it.
A match in standard input has no name before it.
With
.BR -n ,
.I sift
prints each line that a match covers instead, after its name and number,
.IB file : n :\f1.
.PP
.I Sift
finds the leftmost longest match, then looks for the next one after its end, as
.IR sam 's
.B x
command does.
.SS Patterns
A pattern is a list of tokens separated by blanks.
Each item matches one token:
.TF @name{tag}
.TP
.I text
the token whose text is
.IR text .
.TP
.B .
any token.
.TP
.BI @ name
a token one of whose names is
.I name
or begins with
.IB name . \f1;
so
.B @function
matches
.B function
and
.BR function.call .
The names are the program's, as
.IR syn (4)
lists them: a highlight name, such as
.BR keyword ,
.B string
or
.BR variable.parameter ,
and a lexical name for a token no highlight covers, such as
.BR ident ,
.B oper
or
.BR punct .
.TP
.BI @ name { tag }
a token, named as before, inside a range that the program tags
.IR tag ,
such as
.B reference.call
or
.BR definition.function ;
.I tag
matches by its dotted prefix, as
.I name
does.
.TP
.BI / re
a token whose text holds a match of the regular expression
.I re
(see
.IR regexp (6)).
.TP
.BI [ "a b ..." ]
a token that matches one of the items
.IR a ,
.IR b ,
\&... ,
each a text,
.BI @ name
or
.BI / re\f1.
.TP
.BI ^ item
a token that
.I item
does not match.
.TP
.IB x : item
a token that
.I item
matches, bound to the name
.IR x .
.TP
.BI : x
a token with the same text as the one bound to
.IR x .
.PD
.PP
A match binds a name once: a second
.IB x : item
for the same
.I x
matches only a token whose text is the bound one, as
.BI : x
does.
A
.BI : x
that comes before any binding of
.I x
in a match matches nothing, and
.BI ^: x
there matches any token.
.PP
An item followed by
.B *
repeats zero or more times, by
.B \e+
one or more times, and by
.B \e?
zero or one time.
.B \e(
and
.B \e)
group items, and
.B \e|
separates alternatives; it binds more loosely than a list.
So
.B .*
is any run of tokens.
A repetition matches only balanced tokens: for each bracket among them, its partner is among them
too.
So
.B "( .* )"
matches from a parenthesis to its own partner.
The syntax program says which tokens are brackets and pairs them, and
.I sift
never looks at their text: a parenthesis the program leaves unpaired, as in half-typed text, is no
bracket, and a block that only indentation makes has none.
.PP
A backslash before any other character quotes it, so
.B \e*
is the token
.BR * ,
and
.B \e/=
is the token
.BR /= .
A word that is only
.BR * ,
.BR [ ,
.BR ] ,
.B ^
or
.B :
is that token.
.PP
.I Sift
skips tokens named
.B comment
unless the pattern names them, with
.B @comment
or a name that begins
.BR comment. .
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the statements that add one to a variable, whatever the spacing:
.IP
.EX
sift 'x:@ident = :x + 1' *.c
.EE
.PP
Find a block that calls
.I malloc
and never
.I free
after it:
.IP
.EX
sift '{ .* malloc ^free* }' *.c
.EE
.PP
Find the calls inside the function
.IR main ,
as lines:
.IP
.EX
sift -n 'main ( .* ) { .* @function.call{reference.call} ( .* ) .* }' *.c
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF $home/lib/syn
.TP
.B $home/lib/syn
the rules, read in place of
.BR /lib/syn .
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/sift
and
.BR /sys/src/libtok ;
.I tok.md
in its
.B doc
explains the design.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR carve (1),
.IR grep (1),
.IR sam (1),
.IR tok (2),
.IR syn (4)
.br
Gerard Holzmann, Cobra's
.I tgrep
and its
.I pe
command, whose patterns these are.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is
.B "no matches"
if nothing matched, and
.B errors
if
.I sift
could not read or lex a file, or found no rule for it.
.SH BUGS
These patterns differ from Cobra's:
a name matches by its dotted prefix; braces after a name ask for a tag;
every repetition matches only balanced tokens, not
.B .*
alone; and a regular expression must hold no blank.
Cobra's location constraints,
.BI < n >
and
.BI @ n " (" expr ),
are not implemented.
.PP
A regular expression that ends in
.B *
loses it to repetition, as any item does; put the end in a group,
.BR "/(ab*)" .
